WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.907
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.207 --> 00:00:09.977
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.277 --> 00:00:18.073
Unit twenty-three: Speeches and Oratory. Today you will rally an audience with let us, we shall and repeated patterns.

4
00:00:18.373 --> 00:00:21.025
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.967 --> 00:00:25.211
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.005
Speech.

7
00:00:28.017 --> 00:00:30.991
Speech means a formal talk given to an audience.

8
00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:33.594
The speech lasted ten minutes.

9
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:36.720
Your turn. Say: Speech.

10
00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:40.661
Speech.

11
00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:42.534
Oratory.

12
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:46.873
Oratory means the art of speaking skilfully in public.

13
00:00:47.483 --> 00:00:49.518
Great oratory can move a nation.

14
00:00:50.117 --> 00:00:52.769
Your turn. Say: Oratory.

15
00:00:55.633 --> 00:00:56.797
Oratory.

16
00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:58.516
Audience.

17
00:00:59.517 --> 00:01:03.200
Audience means the people who listen to or watch a performance or talk.

18
00:01:03.783 --> 00:01:05.655
The audience stood and applauded.

19
00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.030
Your turn. Say: Audience.

20
00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:13.065
Audience.

21
00:01:13.817 --> 00:01:14.702
Rhetoric.

22
00:01:15.717 --> 00:01:18.668
Rhetoric means the art of using language to persuade.

23
00:01:19.283 --> 00:01:22.014
Skilled rhetoric uses rhythm and contrast.

24
00:01:22.617 --> 00:01:25.536
Your turn. Say: Rhetoric.

25
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.308
Rhetoric.

26
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:31.237
Repetition.

27
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:36.188
Repetition means using the same word or pattern again for effect.

28
00:01:36.783 --> 00:01:39.236
Repetition makes the key idea memorable.

29
00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.560
Your turn. Say: Repetition.

30
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:46.459
Repetition.

31
00:01:47.217 --> 00:01:48.113
Delivery.

32
00:01:49.117 --> 00:01:52.985
Delivery means the way a speaker uses voice, pace and gesture.

33
00:01:53.583 --> 00:01:56.059
Her calm delivery made the message stronger.

34
00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.279
Your turn. Say: Delivery.

35
00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:03.262
Delivery.

36
00:02:04.017 --> 00:02:05.018
Persuasion.

37
00:02:06.017 --> 00:02:09.003
Persuasion means the act of making people agree or act.

38
00:02:09.617 --> 00:02:11.547
Persuasion depends on trust.

39
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.640
Your turn. Say: Persuasion.

40
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:18.559
Persuasion.

41
00:02:19.317 --> 00:02:20.411
Historic.

42
00:02:21.417 --> 00:02:25.134
Historic means important in history and likely to be remembered.

43
00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.471
It was a historic speech.

44
00:02:28.083 --> 00:02:30.538
Your turn. Say: Historic.

45
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:34.355
Historic.

46
00:02:35.167 --> 00:02:38.620
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:39.483 --> 00:02:45.688
Visal, a speaker says 'Let us work together' and 'We shall not rest, we shall not tire.' What makes this powerful?

48
00:02:46.783 --> 00:03:01.248
Two things: inclusive structures and repetition. Let us + base verb invites everyone to act. We shall is formal and firm. Repeating the pattern creates a rhythm that audiences remember.

49
00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:04.117
What is the grammar of 'let us'?

50
00:03:05.217 --> 00:03:17.833
Let us + base verb, with the full form in formal speech: Let us begin. The contraction let's is informal. For negatives, say Let us not forget, or Let's not forget.

51
00:03:18.917 --> 00:03:20.777
How many repeats should I use?

52
00:03:21.883 --> 00:03:33.664
Three is classic. A triad feels complete: We shall not rest, we shall not tire, we shall not fail. Keep each part parallel, short and rising in strength.

53
00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.579
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:37.417 --> 00:03:43.062
Grammar focus: Rallying language: Let us and We shall plus parallel repetition.

55
00:03:43.362 --> 00:03:44.560
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:47.818
Let us work together for a fairer future.

57
00:03:48.717 --> 00:03:51.262
We shall not rest until every child can read.

58
00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:54.614
Let us not forget those who came before us.

59
00:03:55.567 --> 00:04:05.315
Let us + base verb invites; Let us not warns. We shall + base verb is formal and firm. Repeat the pattern three times, with parallel grammar, for rhythm.

60
00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:09.887
Be careful! Don't say: Let's us begin the meeting now.

61
00:04:11.833 --> 00:04:14.309
Say: Let us begin the meeting now.

62
00:04:14.609 --> 00:04:16.608
Use let us or let's, not both.

63
00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.087
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:22.424
The Power of Words.

65
00:04:22.724 --> 00:04:27.266
Oratory is the art of persuading an audience through voice, structure and rhetoric.

66
00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:33.889
A historic speech often uses repetition: we shall learn, we shall build, we shall succeed.

67
00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:38.432
Let us not forget that delivery matters as much as the words themselves.

68
00:04:39.433 --> 00:04:44.405
A calm pace and well-placed pauses help persuasion, because listeners need time to think.

69
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:49.513
When the speech ends, let us ask which lines the audience will remember tomorrow.

70
00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:54.857
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:55.717 --> 00:04:57.925
blank begin with a moment of silence.

72
00:04:58.225 --> 00:05:02.053
A, Let us. B, Let we. C, Let our.

73
00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.541
The answer is A: Let us.

74
00:05:08.683 --> 00:05:11.565
Using the same pattern again for effect is blank.

75
00:05:11.865 --> 00:05:15.716
A, delivery. B, repetition. C, oratory.

76
00:05:19.267 --> 00:05:21.510
The answer is B: repetition.

77
00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:24.220
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:30.263
A, Let us to hope for peace.. B, Let us hope for peace.. C, Let us hoping for peace..

79
00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.543
The answer is B: Let us hope for peace..

80
00:05:37.683 --> 00:05:41.018
The way a speaker uses voice, pace and gesture is blank.

81
00:05:41.318 --> 00:05:45.238
A, rhetoric. B, delivery. C, persuasion.

82
00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:50.649
The answer is B: delivery.

83
00:05:51.783 --> 00:05:54.212
According to the reading, what do listeners need?

84
00:05:54.512 --> 00:05:59.002
A, time to think. B, a longer speech. C, a printed copy.

85
00:06:02.567 --> 00:06:04.868
The answer is A: time to think.

86
00:06:06.067 --> 00:06:09.369
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:10.217 --> 00:06:13.110
Which famous speech do you find most powerful?

88
00:06:21.917 --> 00:06:23.394
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:23.694 --> 00:06:45.450
I find the speeches about freedom and equality the most powerful, because they use clear words, repetition and hope. The speaker does not only inform; he or she invites us to act. The delivery is calm but passionate, and the audience feels part of the message. Let us remember that good words can change history.

90
00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.459
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:54.787
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and rallying language: let us and we shall plus parallel repetition.

92
00:06:55.087 --> 00:07:00.073
Today's value is eloquence. Choose your words with care, and speak to persuade honestly.

93
00:07:00.373 --> 00:07:03.211
See you in Unit twenty-four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:03.511 --> 00:07:06.825
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

